module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.unapplied_generic_accumulator_types_element_as_container

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data unapplied_generic_accumulator_types_element_as_container: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "unapplied_generic_accumulator_types_element_as_container" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "INVALID STATE: a value of a generic container reaches an algebra receiver typed as the container's UNAPPLIED declaration -- the `FreeMonoid` expansion (a Disj whose children are its variants, still carrying params [T]) -- although the element type is fixed by authored facts at that site. v1.compiler.infer_types algebra_child_or_placeholder then reads the argument positionally off the expansion, and for a coproduct the position holds a variant, so the ELEMENT IS TYPED AS THE CONTAINER. The advisory AlgebraApplicationEvidenceUnavailable is raised, but the mistyped element flows on.",

    "HARM, measured on main 7af52ea7e2 with an entry compile of a scratch probe: `let carried = fold(xs, init: Empty, f: fn(acc, e) { list_snoc_item(xs: acc, item: e) })` then `fold(carried, init: 0, f: fn(acc, t) { acc + t.v })` raises the advisory AND refuses `no field 'v' on type 'FreeMonoid'` -- a false refusal of correct code. Where nothing reads a field the same mistyping is SILENT: src/v2/std/type_binder.dag type_param_names folds over a `carried` built exactly this way and its `t` is typed as the container. The annotated twin (`let carried: List<Item> = ...`) is clean in both respects, which is the evidence that the argument was present and dropped, not absent.",

    "DISTINGUISHING FACTS. Two producers drop the argument, and they are different links. (1) FOLD ACCUMULATOR: the fold call's existing refinement of the accumulator from the step's return (v1.compiler.infer refined_call_fold_acc_type) fires only when is_fully_resolved says the accumulator is under-resolved, and an unapplied generic expansion reads as fully resolved. (2) VARIANT LITERAL: `Cons { head: c.head, tail: c.tail }` with no expected type is typed by infer_record_lit_structural as the parent's declaration, uninstantiated from its field values. Live site: dag/gunbc/spark/host_commitment.dag spark_commitment_causes_text. Not distinguishing: whether the class is bisectable to a recent landing -- both live sites already fire at 1f1feb26d2 (#12240, 2026-09-25), and the census surfaced the class only once an earlier census refusal stopped masking it.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: outside the ladder for the silent element mistyping (counted only as a non-blocking advisory, and the advisory's own note said the evidence did not exist). RUNG NOW: producer (1) is repaired at its link -- fold_accumulator_is_unapplied_generic makes the existing refinement fire -- and its red and control are enrolled in test.claim.unapplied_generic_accumulator_witness_test, so on that path the mistyping is structurally guaranteed absent for the measured shape. Producer (2) is unchanged and stays outside the ladder at one live site; the class's minimum across paths is therefore still outside the ladder, and this row says so rather than citing the repaired path.",

    "CEILING: structurally guaranteed (the argument is decidable from authored facts at every measured site). NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, named as the capability: a coproduct-variant record literal produces a nominal application carrying its inferred type arguments (the grounding gunbc.guarantee_stall.algebra_expansion_positional_argument_stall names), whose construction is derived-node identity step 2 (gunbc.recurring_failure_mode generic_identity_decided_by_spelling), because instantiating the literal mints an applied type node and minting one by parameter name is the spelling-keyed construction that step retires. SUFFICIENT FOR: host_commitment's `Cons { .. }` receiver to carry its argument, so AlgebraApplicationEvidenceUnavailable fires at zero corpus sites.",

    "ADJACENT AND NOT THIS CLASS, recorded because the review control asked for it and it did not hold: a fold whose `init` is an APPLIED `List<A>` and whose step pushes a `B`, or whose result is declared `List<B>`, is ADMITTED on main and on this change alike. That is a missing conformance judgment between the step's return and the accumulator, not a dropped argument; this change does not alter its verdict (the refinement fires only for an unapplied accumulator, so an applied binding is never overwritten), and it is not repaired here.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
